A fault on a thread Wine didn't create ends the program instead of hanging it.

Apple's libdispatch workers run Metal and D3DMetal code inside the game's
process, on threads without a TEB. When such a thread faults, segv_handler
(and trap_handler, fpe_handler) runs on it, and save_context reads the TEB's
copy of the debug registers first: it faults at 0x2f0 while SIGSEGV is still
blocked, macOS retries that fault forever, and the faulting thread spins while
the rest of the process carries on with no frame drawn. GravityMark 1.89
-direct3d12 on D3DMetal 4.0 beta 3 (macOS 27, M2 Max) hangs this way from about
155,500 asteroids up: the first fault is a NULL read at 0x14 in
-[AGXG14XFamilyResidencySet _commitAddedAllocations:count:removedAllocations:count:]
under D3DMetal's command replayer (Apple's bug, reported separately; #23).

On macOS each handler now checks NtCurrentTeb() first. Without a TEB it puts
the signal back to its default action and returns: the faulting load runs
again and the process ends with SIGSEGV (a trap, which isn't retried, is raised
again; SIGFPE likewise), so the crash report names the real fault. A fault Wine
handles on a thread with a TEB takes the same path as before.

Checked on an M2 Max with the r29 ntdll.so rebuilt with this patch:
GravityMark -direct3d12 at 200,000 asteroids exits with SIGSEGV after 7 s
instead of hanging; -direct3d11 scores the same as before (9,594 against
9,653-9,731). Not sent upstream.

--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/signal_x86_64.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/signal_x86_64.c
@@ -2506,6 +2506,32 @@ static inline BOOL check_invalid_gsbase( ucontext_t *ucontext )
 }
 
 
+#ifdef __APPLE__
+/**********************************************************************
+ *		foreign_thread_fault
+ *
+ * A fault on a thread Wine didn't create, which has no TEB: Apple's libdispatch workers running
+ * Metal and D3DMetal code are such threads. The handlers below read the TEB right away, fault
+ * again and re-enter themselves forever, so the program hangs at 100% CPU instead of crashing.
+ * Put the signal back to its default action instead: a fault happens again when the faulting
+ * instruction is retried (a trap, which isn't retried, is raised again), and the process crashes
+ * with a crash report that names the real fault.
+ */
+static BOOL foreign_thread_fault( int sig, BOOL retried )
+{
+    struct sigaction sa;
+
+    if (NtCurrentTeb()) return FALSE;
+    memset( &sa, 0, sizeof(sa) );
+    sa.sa_handler = SIG_DFL;
+    sigemptyset( &sa.sa_mask );
+    sigaction( sig, &sa, NULL );
+    if (!retried) raise( sig );  /* blocked while this handler runs: delivered when it returns */
+    return TRUE;
+}
+#endif
+
+
 /**********************************************************************
  *		segv_handler
  *
@@ -2517,6 +2543,10 @@ static void segv_handler( int signal, siginfo_t *siginfo, void *sigcontext )
     EXCEPTION_RECORD rec = { 0 };
     struct xcontext context;
 
+#ifdef __APPLE__
+    if (foreign_thread_fault( signal, TRUE )) return;
+#endif
+
     rec.ExceptionAddress = (void *)RIP_sig(ucontext);
     save_context( &context, ucontext );
 
@@ -2613,6 +2643,10 @@ static void trap_handler( int signal, siginfo_t *siginfo, void *sigcontext )
     EXCEPTION_RECORD rec = { 0 };
     struct xcontext context;
 
+#ifdef __APPLE__
+    if (foreign_thread_fault( signal, FALSE )) return;
+#endif
+
     if (handle_syscall_trap( ucontext, siginfo )) return;
 
     rec.ExceptionAddress = (void *)RIP_sig(ucontext);
@@ -2647,6 +2681,10 @@ static void fpe_handler( int signal, siginfo_t *siginfo, void *sigcontext )
     EXCEPTION_RECORD rec = { .ExceptionAddress = (void *)RIP_sig(ucontext) };
     struct xcontext context;
 
+#ifdef __APPLE__
+    if (foreign_thread_fault( signal, TRUE )) return;
+#endif
+
     save_context( &context, sigcontext );
 
     switch (siginfo->si_code)
